WEBVTT

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:03.604
Hidden timber condition needs inspection evidence.

00:00:05.346 --> 00:00:09.829
The U B one thousand uses opposed devices to send and receive ultrasound

00:00:09.843 --> 00:00:10.712
through the pole.

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:13.770
Here they mark one inspection plane.

00:00:14.707 --> 00:00:18.960
The laboratory animation is illustrative; it is not a calibrated instrument

00:00:18.973 --> 00:00:19.435
reading.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:25.817
The Axonic Analyser prototype organises the inspection around a pole

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:26.741
and its scans.

00:00:28.483 --> 00:00:32.233
The scan view brings the devices and acquisition workflow together.

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:36.839
In this demonstration, example signals drive the display.

00:00:37.776 --> 00:00:41.173
No connected instrument or measured field result is implied.

00:00:43.033 --> 00:00:47.028
A scan produces evidence to examine, rather than a picture of the exact

00:00:47.041 --> 00:00:47.897
decay shape.

00:00:49.639 --> 00:00:52.887
The analyser keeps scan information available for review.

00:00:53.824 --> 00:00:57.724
Signal behaviour needs an approved interpretation; a single peak is

00:00:57.737 --> 00:01:00.522
not a direct measurement of pole bending capacity.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.841
Records connect the inspection activity with the pole being assessed.

00:01:08.583 --> 00:01:13.257
Keep that evidence alongside material, geometry and structural information.

00:01:14.195 --> 00:01:18.298
The demonstration shows the workflow, while the laboratory uses a separate

00:01:18.311 --> 00:01:19.113
bending model.

00:01:20.051 --> 00:01:24.249
Neither the animation nor these example screens grants climbing clearance.
